## Core Idea

A node is:

- **Safe** if every path from it eventually stops at a terminal node.
- **Unsafe** if it is part of a cycle or can reach a cycle.

```text
Can reach a cycle     → Unsafe
Cannot reach a cycle  → Safe
```

## DFS States

```text
0 = Unvisited
1 = Currently in the DFS path
2 = Confirmed safe
3 = Confirmed unsafe
```

If DFS reaches a node with `state[node] == 1`, that node is already in the current path, so we found a cycle.

## Java Code

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public List<Integer> eventualSafeNodes(int[][] graph) {
        int n = graph.length;

        // 0 = unvisited, 1 = current path
        // 2 = safe, 3 = unsafe
        int[] state = new int[n];

        List<Integer> safeNodes = new ArrayList<>();

        // Check every node
        for (int node = 0; node < n; node++) {
            if (dfs(node, graph, state)) {
                safeNodes.add(node);
            }
        }

        return safeNodes;
    }

    private boolean dfs(int node, int[][] graph, int[] state) {

        // Node is already in the current path → cycle
        if (state[node] == 1) {
            return false;
        }

        // Already confirmed safe
        if (state[node] == 2) {
            return true;
        }

        // Already confirmed unsafe
        if (state[node] == 3) {
            return false;
        }

        // Add node to current DFS path
        state[node] = 1;

        // Check every neighbor
        for (int neighbor : graph[node]) {

            // One unsafe neighbor makes this node unsafe
            if (!dfs(neighbor, graph, state)) {
                state[node] = 3;
                return false;
            }
        }

        // Every neighbor is safe
        state[node] = 2;
        return true;
    }
}
```

## Important Logic

```java
for (int neighbor : graph[node]) {
    if (!dfs(neighbor, graph, state)) {
        state[node] = 3;
        return false;
    }
}
```

Meaning:

```text
Any neighbor unsafe → Current node unsafe
All neighbors safe  → Current node safe
```

## Why `graph[node]`?

Because the input is:

```java
int[][] graph
```

```text
Array       → graph[node]
ArrayList   → graph.get(node)
```

## Complexity

```text
Time:  O(V + E)
Space: O(V)
```

## Memory Line

> Mark the node as currently exploring, check all its neighbors, and mark it safe only if every neighbor is safe.
